{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Boolean logic operates with binary variables (true/false)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:58:16\u201300:59:03]: 'Boolean logic operates just in Boolean variables, so either true or false.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Modus ponens is the main inference rule in Boolean logic",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:58:16\u201300:59:03]: 'the main rule for logical inference is so-called modus ponens. That states the following. If A is true, and you will know that B falls from A, we can conclude that B is true as well.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Boolean logic cannot infer A from A → B and B being true",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:59:04\u201300:59:53]: 'from a Boolean logic point of view, we cannot say anything because we can derive two either from two or from false.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "In the football example, A = 'Werder Bremen wins the match' and B = 'Werder fans are celebrating'",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [00:59:53\u201301:00:26] and [01:00:27\u201301:00:57]: 'So I know that if there are no wins, this is A, then the football fans will definitely be celebrating. So this is B.' and 'I know that A implies B. I know that B is true.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian reasoning uses joint distributions to update probabilities",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [01:02:02\u201301:02:44]: 'we may switch the probabilities. And we may say that let us establish joint distribution of A and B, and then we can easily derive P of A given B.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution P(A,B) = P(B|A) * P(A)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [01:02:44\u201301:03:15]: 'And this joint distribution can be, for example, be written as PoB given A times PoA.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Prior probability P(A) = 0.1 for Werder winning the match",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [01:02:44\u201301:03:15]: 'So prior probability of A was, say, 1.0, 0.1.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Posterior probability P(A|B) > P(A) after observing B",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [01:02:44\u201301:03:15]: 'probably, well, probably clearly the posterior probability that worder has won the game will be larger than my prior probability when I observed people who are celebrating and waving the worder flex.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR has some errors (e.g., 'worder' instead of 'Werder', 'PoB' instead of 'P(B)', '1.0, 0.1' for prior probability). The timestamped evidence is accurate but the transcription has minor errors."
